\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{The reduced sum on the two-by-two sign torus}
\date{2026-11-19}

\p{On the labeled torus of \ref{fdip-010H}, the [reduced
links](fdip-010X) supply #{8} independent signs. At each positive
link the factor is #{(3+V_e)/2}. At each of the #{4} faces it is
#{(3+V_f)/2}, where #{V_f} is its sign product.}

\p{Expand the #{12} factors and select a set #{A} of faces.
Let #{E_A} be their odd boundary, the labeled edges occurring
an odd number of times. The sum over signs kills every monomial
except the selection of precisely #{E_A} among the link factors.
Each surviving monomial is one at all #{2^8} link configurations.
Its contribution to #{\check Z} is therefore
#{2^{-4}3^{12-|A|-|E_A|}}. The empty and full face sets have
empty boundary. The #{4} singletons and their #{4} complements
have boundary size #{4}. Among the pairs, the #{4} neighboring
pairs each share two edges and leave #{4} boundary edges.
The two diagonal pairs leave #{8}. Thus
##{\begin{aligned}
16\check Z&=3^{12}+4\cdot3^7+4\cdot3^6\\
 &\quad+2\cdot3^2+4\cdot3^5+3^8,\\
\check Z&=34416.
\end{aligned}}
The #{4} independent site frames in \ref{fdip-010Y} give
#{Z=16\check Z=550656}, agreeing with the direct sum over sites
and links.}
